/-!
# Domination of e-processes for the one-sided conditionally sub-Gaussian null by predictable
products of mixtures of exponentials

The one-sided conditionally `1`-sub-Gaussian null on `ℕ → ℝ` is the set of probability measures
under which, for every `k` and every `l ≥ 0`,
`E[exp (l X_k - l ^ 2 / 2) | X_0, …, X_{k - 1}] ≤ 1`, stated through set integrals over the sets
measurable for the observations before `k` (`MeasureTheory.Filtration.naturalLT`). It is the
sequential null of infinitely many conditional constraints `exp (l x - l ^ 2 / 2) - 1`, `l ≥ 0`.

Every e-process for this null and the canonical filtration `Filtration.piLE` is dominated, at
every time `n` and on every path `ω`, by a product
`∏ k ≤ n, ∫ exp (l ω_k - l ^ 2 / 2) ∂π_k(l)` of mixtures of the exponentials, whose mixing
measures `π k` on `[0, ∞)` have mass at most `1` and depend only on the observations before `k`
[EVR26, Note 12, Theorem 4]. This is the sequential form of the domination of e-variables for
the one-sided sub-Gaussian null by mixtures of exponentials [LRR26, Theorem 4.3], for the
question of [Cle26b] and [LRR26, §10] on sequential complete classes for infinitely many
constraints. No measurability of the mixing measures in the past observations is asserted, so the
dominating product need not be a measurable process.

The domination holds for every process that only depends at time `n` on the observations up to
`n`, with no measurability, whose mean at every bounded stopping time is at most `1` under every
finitely supported member of the null.

## Main definitions

* `ProbabilityTheory.oneSidedCondSubgaussianNull`: the one-sided conditionally `1`-sub-Gaussian
  null on `ℕ → ℝ`.
* `ProbabilityTheory.oneSidedSubgaussianNull`: the one-sided `1`-sub-Gaussian null on `ℝ`, the
  null of one round, generated by the constraints `exp (l x - l ^ 2 / 2) - 1`, `l ≥ 0`
  (`ProbabilityTheory.constraintNull`).

## Main statements

* `ProbabilityTheory.exists_le_lintegral_exp_of_forall_lintegral_le_one`: one step, the
  domination of every function whose mean is at most `1` under the finitely supported members of
  `oneSidedSubgaussianNull` by a mixture of exponentials [LRR26, Theorem 4.3].
* `ProbabilityTheory.exists_le_prod_lintegral_exp_of_forall_lintegral_le_one`: the domination of
  every process that is valid at bounded stopping times under the finitely supported members of
  the null.
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsEProcess.exists_le_prod_lintegral_exp`: the domination of every e-process
  for the null.

## Implementation notes

The statements and proofs are adapted from `EValuesResearch/Results/SubGaussianCompleteClass.lean`
and `EValuesResearch/Results/SubGaussianCompleteClassAux.lean` (commit `dd4072f`) in the paper
project `RemyDegenne/evalues_research`, which formalizes [EVR26, Note 12, Theorem 4].

*One step.* A function `f : ℝ → ℝ≥0∞` whose mean is at most `1` under every finitely supported
probability measure `μ` with `∫ exp (l x - l ^ 2 / 2) ∂μ ≤ 1` for `l ≥ 0` is dominated by a
mixture of the exponentials with a mixing measure of mass at most `1`. On a finite set of points,
a separating functional otherwise gives a finitely supported measure of mass below `1` with
exponential means below `1` under which `f` has mean above `1`; shrinking it slightly and adding
a far negative atom turns it into a probability measure of the one-step null. The mixing measures
of mass at most `1` on the compact space `EReal` form a compact set
(`isCompact_setOfPred_finiteMeasure_le_of_compactSpace`), and the finite intersection property
gives one mixing measure for all points.

*Induction.* The envelope after a history of length `k` is the largest mean of the process at a
bounded stopping time, under a finitely supported member of the null continuing that history.
At the root it is at most `1`. Grafting finitely many continuations shows that its mean after
one more observation, under every finitely supported law of the one-step null, is at most its
current value. The one-step domination applied to the ratio of two consecutive envelopes gives
the mixing measure of the next round, chosen by the axiom of choice for every history.

## References

* [Cle26b] E. Clerico, *Sequential testing of conditionally constrained hypotheses*,
  arXiv:2606.06769v2.
* [EVR26] *Answers to open questions in e-values and anytime-valid inference*, notes 01 to 34 and
  the Lean library `EValuesResearch` of github.com/RemyDegenne/evalues_research, 2026; written and
  refereed by AI agents.
* [LRR26] M. Larsson, A. Ramdas and J. Ruf, *Testing hypotheses generated by constraints*, Math.
  Oper. Res. (2026), arXiv:2504.02974v5.
-/

